<Project>
    <!--
        One check, and it exists because the failure it replaces names nothing.

        Google.Cloud.Functions.Hosting ships the target that writes a function's Main in build/
        rather than buildTransitive/, and NuGet imports build/ for a direct reference only. So a
        project that reaches that package through this one - which is the ordinary way to arrive,
        since this package depends on it - gets the assemblies and not the target, and the build
        fails with

            error CS5001: Program does not contain a static 'Main' method suitable for an entry point

        which says nothing about Cloud Functions and nothing about what to add. Measured on a
        consumer built from the released packages; it is the first thing anyone following the
        readme will hit.

        A warning rather than an error: a project may legitimately write its own Main and call
        Google.Cloud.Functions.Hosting.EntryPoint.StartAsync from it, and the assembly reference for
        that does flow transitively. What is not legitimate is arriving here by accident.

        OutputType=Exe, so a library or a test project consuming this package is silent.

        AutoGenerateEntryPoint is the property Google's own props sets to true, so an empty value is
        exactly "those assets were not imported". A project that sets it to false to keep its own
        Main - which is how one assembly serves both Google deployment models, a Cloud Run container
        and a 2nd gen function, from one compiled application - has set it, and is silent too.

        buildTransitive/, and Package\ in the source tree, for the reasons the
        Hardened.Gcp.CloudRun.PubSub csproj gives.
    -->
    <Target Name="HardenedCloudFunctionsEntryPointCheck"
            BeforeTargets="PrepareForBuild"
            Condition="'$(OutputType)' == 'Exe' AND '$(AutoGenerateEntryPoint)' == ''">
        <Warning Code="HRDGF002"
                 Text="This project references Hardened.Gcp.Functions.Runtime but not Google.Cloud.Functions.Hosting, so the Functions Framework entry point is not generated and the build has no Main. Add &lt;PackageReference Include=&quot;Google.Cloud.Functions.Hosting&quot; /&gt;, or set AutoGenerateEntryPoint to false if this project supplies its own." />
    </Target>
</Project>
